#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048252 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050163 :topic/math-academy-id 1167 :topic/title "Index Notation for Matrices"}] :topic/id #uuid "8c876864-a853-56d6-a887-91d7f8f1cf14" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087695 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087502 :question/id #uuid "9c82456c-49ea-5a5e-b0a2-0ab17059acee" :question/math-academy-id "e-4390" :question/problem "Which of the following are zero matrices?\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- $P$ is a zero matrix since *all* its entries are zeros.\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a zero matrix since some of its entries are not zeros (for instance, $q_{12}=0.1\\neq0$).\n\n- $R$ is a zero matrix since *all* its entries are zeros.\n\nTherefore, only $P$ and $R$ are zero matrices."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Zero Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087696 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087503 :question/id #uuid "bcb710d0-a687-5686-ae3a-0d0773857530" :question/math-academy-id "e-4391" :question/problem "Which of the following are square matrices?\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- $U$ is *not* a square matrix since the numbers of rows and columns are not the same. It has $2$ rows and $3$ columns, so it's a $2 \\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- $W$ is a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. It has $3$ rows and $3$ columns, so it is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- $Z$ is a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. It has $2$ rows and $2$ columns, so it is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix.\n\nTherefore, $W$ and $Z$ are square matrices."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Square Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087697 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087504 :question/id #uuid "79974103-2da9-599a-b33b-abac3d32613a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4392" :question/problem "Which of the following are diagonal matrices?\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- $Q$ is a diagonal matrix. It's a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. Also, all the entries that do not lie on the main diagonal are zeros.\n\nNotice that $Q$ is not an identity matrix since the diagonal entries are not all ones.\n\n- $R$ is *not* a diagonal matrix. It's a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. But some of the entries that do not lie on the main diagonal are not zeros (for instance, $r_{13}=1 \\neq 0$).\n\n- $S$ is a diagonal matrix. It's a square matrix since it has the same number of rows and columns. Also, all the entries that do not lie on the main diagonal are zeros.\n\nNotice that $S$ is not an identity matrix since the diagonal entries are not all ones.\n\nTherefore, only $Q$ and $S$ are diagonal matrices."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Special Square Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087698 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087505 :question/id #uuid "0e3ad3cd-f7c4-55f4-8a0b-b850283bc6b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4393" :question/problem "How can we best describe the following matrix?\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $F$ is a matrix that has a single column. Therefore, it is a column vector."} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Other Matrices"}] :topic/math-academy-id 862 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048297 :topic/math-academy-id 147 :topic/title "Multiplying Square Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050283 :topic/math-academy-id 1777 :topic/title "Triangular Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825 :topic/math-academy-id 1195 :topic/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column Vector"}] :topic/title "Zero, Square, Diagonal and Identity Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049162 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049051 :topic/math-academy-id 2310 :topic/title "Compound Inequalities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050015 :topic/math-academy-id 3709 :topic/title "Simplifying Expressions With Surds"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050404 :topic/math-academy-id 2204 :topic/title "Surds"}] :topic/id #uuid "df4f6208-5d6c-5dc9-bd38-c7d85e32fb73" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059385 :question/id #uuid "9096883e-6dd0-5dd1-8e9c-176775edadba" :question/math-academy-id "e-10785" :question/problem "Which inequality corresponds to the interval $x \\in (1, 5)?$" :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur interval contains only parentheses. So, the endpoints are *not* included. \n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{red}{(}}1, 5{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our interval can be expressed as the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1 \\,\\,{\\color{red}{<      }}\\,\\,      x  \\,\\,{\\color{red}{<      }}\\,\\,      5\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Intervals and Inequalities: Open Intervals"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059827 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059386 :question/id #uuid "b828f144-b8b8-5eda-94f1-43cccdcb1aaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-10786" :question/problem "Write the interval $y\\in\\big(-4, \\sqrt{2}\\big]$ as an inequality." :question/worked-solution "Every interval can be expressed as an inequality. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ to indicate that the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0}\\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ to indicate that the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given interval \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{{\\big(}}} -4, \\sqrt2\\, {\\color{blue}{\\big]}}\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $-4$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,.$\n\n- The right endpoint $\\sqrt2$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{blue}{\\leq      }}\\,.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is \n\n$$\n-4 \\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\lt  }}\\,\\,   y \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\leq}}\\,\\, \\sqrt 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Half-Open Intervals and Inequalities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059828 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059387 :question/id #uuid "45d05c7d-e278-552f-a524-926d4a57d870" :question/math-academy-id "e-10787" :question/problem "What interval corresponds to the segment shown below?\n\n![](images/43/430481f9d0863b0bb5602f949e6f994d8007ea29fdd741a485d880df8d5d2dec.png)" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nThe given interval can be represented by the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-2\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\leq}}  \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{red}{\\lt}} \\,\\, 2\n$$\n\nWe can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\in {\\color{blue}{[}}-2,2{\\color{red}{)}}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Translating Between Half-Open Intervals and Number Lines"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059829 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059388 :question/id #uuid "6d4bd0a2-0961-54c1-b781-b470d420d848" :question/math-academy-id "e-10816" :question/problem "Express the inequality $1 \\le x \\le4$ in interval notation." :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses ${\\color{red}{(}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{red}{)}}$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets ${\\color{blue}{[}}\\phantom{0} \\phantom{0}{\\color{blue}{]}}$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $1$ and $4$ *are* included. \n\n$$\n1\\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, x \\,\\, {\\color{blue}{\\le}} \\,\\, 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{[}}1,4{\\color{blue}{]}}\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [1,4].\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Converting Between Intervals and Inequalities: Closed Intervals"}] :topic/math-academy-id 239 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048793 :topic/math-academy-id 6287 :topic/title "Optimizing Sums, Differences, Products, and Quotients"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050301 :topic/math-academy-id 4028 :topic/title "Unbounded Intervals"}] :topic/title "Interval Notation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049304 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896 :topic/math-academy-id 861 :topic/title "Introduction to Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049626 :topic/math-academy-id 297 :topic/title "Subtracting a Negative Number"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050421 :topic/math-academy-id 328 :topic/title "Adding a Negative Number"}] :topic/id #uuid "eb12f285-e65a-58f1-8269-2aaa56459015" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054648 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054545 :question/id #uuid "45832301-e578-578d-8866-147b998a1c22" :question/math-academy-id "e-4374" :question/problem "Calculate $A+B,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-10 & -5+2 \\\\ -2+2 & 3-4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Adding Square Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054649 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054546 :question/id #uuid "fc4a66f3-5180-5ac0-bd4d-830196fa459a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4375" :question/problem "Calculate $A-B,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We subtract the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-B & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-0 & -1-(-1) \\\\ -1-2 & 0-(-1) \\\\ 3-(-1) & 2-3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Subtracting Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054650 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054547 :question/id #uuid "b77816bd-e128-5888-9973-97c51c99f7f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4396" :question/problem "Calculate ${A}+ {B},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+(-2) & 3+(-3) & 1+1 & 0+1 \\\\ 2+1 & 4+(-2) & -1+3 & 2+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Adding Non-Square Matrices"}] :topic/math-academy-id 29 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048863 :topic/math-academy-id 1168 :topic/title "Properties of Matrix Addition"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050979 :topic/math-academy-id 146 :topic/title "Scalar Multiplication of Matrices"}] :topic/title "Adding and Subtracting Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050136 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048816 :topic/math-academy-id 1165 :topic/title "Two-Dimensional Vectors Expressed in Component Form"}] :topic/id #uuid "2f9e7fe2-15c2-5053-ac76-14e670eead2b" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059710 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059523 :question/id #uuid "1d2717f9-b905-59eb-85d7-6db4f9c9dc7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4292" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}$ if $\\mathbf{a}=-4\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=12\\mathbf{i}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}$, we just need to compute all of the $\\mathbf{i}$'s and $\\mathbf{j}$'s, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na - 2b &= (-4i + 6j) - 2(12i) \\\\\n&=-4i + 6j - 24i \\\\\n&= (-4i - 24i) + 6j \\\\\n&=-28i + 6j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Addition and Scalar Multiplication of Vectors in Component Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059711 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059524 :question/id #uuid "2cb63c87-320a-5cb4-b413-8c6bf9723641" :question/math-academy-id "e-4293" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -7, 15 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 14, -11 \\rangle$. Find $\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b}$, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na - 3b &= ⟨ - 7, 15⟩ - 3 \\cdot ⟨14,-11⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 7, 15⟩ - ⟨42,-33⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 7 - 42, 15 - (-33)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 49, 48⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Sums and Scalar Multiplication of Vectors Using Angle Bracket Notation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059712 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059525 :question/id #uuid "43cfef07-255d-5167-b815-267883918993" :question/math-academy-id "e-4294" :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$ $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$ Find $2\\mathbf{a}-(\\mathbf{b}-5\\mathbf{c})$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a - (b - 5c) &= 2 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} - (\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} - 5 \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} - (\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ -31\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Sums and Scalar Multiplication of Column Vectors"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059713 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059526 :question/id #uuid "0adf8fad-9a6f-5eb1-970f-11633ba98ca1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4295" :question/problem "The vectors $\\vec{a}$ and $\\vec{b}$ are given in the picture below. Find the coordinates of $\\vec{a}+6\\vec{b}$.\n\n![](images/56/56b10e759084da6fb21c8421bc9ff6d74aac2602df28f6c8e478bd874d7d6e9f.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the picture, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\vec{a}=\\langle -3,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\vec{b}=\\langle 4,5 \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, to compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^(→) + 6b^(→) &= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + 6 \\cdot ⟨4, 5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + ⟨6 \\cdot 4, 6 \\cdot 5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3, 2⟩ + ⟨24, 30⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨21, 32⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Sums and Scalar Multiplication of Vectors Given in Alternative Notation"}] :topic/math-academy-id 244 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048941 :topic/math-academy-id 241 :topic/title "Calculating the Direction of Cartesian Vectors in 2D"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049537 :topic/math-academy-id 175 :topic/title "Addition and Scalar Multiplication of Cartesian Vectors in 3D"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050466 :topic/math-academy-id 2242 :topic/title "Arithmetic in the Complex Plane"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050988 :topic/math-academy-id 176 :topic/title "Calculating the Magnitude of Cartesian Vectors in 2D"}] :topic/title "Addition and Scalar Multiplication of Cartesian Vectors in 2D"}]}
